Now let me design the implementation.

Requirements:
1. `find_entity(author: dict)` must attempt author resolution in priority order: name + birth/death dates, alternate_names + birth/death dates, surname + birth/death dates.
2. When both birth_date and death_date present in input, they must disambiguate; exact year match for both takes precedence.
3. When either birth/death date absent, fall back to case-insensitive name matching alone; return existing author if one exists under that name.
4. Case-insensitive matching; different casings resolve to same record.
5. Wildcards in name patterns: `"John*"` must return first candidate according to numeric key ordering (key_int). If no match, return a new author candidate preserving the input name including `*`.
6. alternate_names match requires both birth_date and death_date present in the input and exactly matching candidate's values when dates are available.
7. surname match requires both dates present and exactly matching; surname path must not resolve if either date missing or mismatched.
8. No valid match → new author candidate dict returned, preserving `name`, `birth_date`, `death_date` unchanged.
9. Year comparison for birth/death dates considers only the year component; any year difference invalidates a match.
10. `find_author(author: dict)` accepts the author import dict and returns a list of candidate author records consistent with the matching rules; `find_entity(author: dict)` delegates to `find_author` and returns either an existing record or None.
11. Mock query behavior must replicate production ILIKE semantics: case-insensitive full-string matching, `*` = multi-char wildcard, `_` ignored in patterns. Interface: `MockSite.regex_ilike(self, pattern, text) -> bool` in `openlibrary/mocks/mock_infobase.py`.

